<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>DrakeForge standalone launcher</title>
<style>
  :root { color-scheme: dark; }
  body {
    margin: 0;
    min-height: 100vh;
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    padding: 24px;
    box-sizing: border-box;
    background: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 10%, #4a0000 0, #0a0a0a 55%, #050505 100%);
    color: #f0f0f0;
    font: 15px/1.55 system-ui, sans-serif;
  }
  main {
    max-width: 38rem;
    padding: 28px;
    border: 1px solid #2a2a2a;
    border-radius: 12px;
    background: rgba(13, 13, 13, 0.92);
    box-shadow: 0 0 28px rgba(230, 0, 18, 0.18);
  }
  .eyebrow {
    margin: 0 0 8px;
    color: #ff4d5a;
    font: 700 11px/1.2 system-ui, sans-serif;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }
  h1 { margin: 0 0 14px; font-size: 22px; }
  p { margin: 0 0 14px; color: #aaaaaa; }
  strong { color: #f0f0f0; }
  a {
    display: inline-block;
    margin: 8px 0 14px;
    padding: 10px 16px;
    border-radius: 6px;
    background: linear-gradient(135deg, #e60012, #4a0000);
    color: #fff;
    font-weight: 700;
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  code { color: #ff4d5a; }
  .note { margin-bottom: 0; font-size: 12px; color: #666666; }
</style>
</head>
<body>
<!--
  DrakeForge launcher for Dragon86 — NOT an embedded IDE.

  This file is a pointer only. It opens DrakeForge's existing standalone
  BrowserCode route at /DrakeForge/standalone in a separate top-level browser
  tab. It deliberately contains no iframe, no BrowserCode/IDE components, and
  no scripts. The standalone page owns its own BrowserPod runtime, 9p Worker
  and COOP/COEP service worker; Dragon86 and the PS1 emulator are not required
  to become cross-origin isolated.

  GitHub Pages cannot set per-route COOP/COEP response headers, so DrakeForge
  uses a project-scoped coi-serviceworker registration in the new tab. Install
  this shortcut as drakeforge.d86 in ~/applications so Dragon86's app registry
  launches it; the Files app's sandboxed HTML preview does not allow the popped
  tab to escape its sandbox restrictions.
-->
<main>
  <p class="eyebrow">DrakeForge · Dragon86 launcher</p>
  <h1>Open the standalone BrowserCode workspace</h1>
  <p>
    This launcher opens <strong>DrakeForge standalone</strong> in a
    <strong>separate top-level browser tab</strong>. It does not embed the IDE in
    an iframe and does not copy its UI into Dragon86.
  </p>
  <p>
    The destination is the existing
    <code>https://dragon-gaming-platforms.github.io/DrakeForge/standalone</code>
    page: the same BrowserPod workspace, terminal, agents, Git, editor and 9p-backed
    files. Dragon86 and the PS1 emulator stay in this window; they are not made
    cross-origin isolated. The new tab establishes the COOP/COEP isolation that
    BrowserPod's <code>SharedArrayBuffer</code> needs in its own browsing context.
  </p>
  <a
    id="drakeforge-launch"
    href="https://dragon-gaming-platforms.github.io/DrakeForge/standalone"
    target="_blank"
    rel="noopener noreferrer"
    data-launch-mode="separate-top-level-tab"
  >Open DrakeForge in a new tab</a>
  <p class="note">
    Standalone mode uses its own in-browser 9p Worker and IndexedDB. It does not
    connect to Dragon86's <code>fs1</code> filesystem. If this file opens as a
    sandboxed HTML preview instead of an app shortcut, use Dragon86's app
    launcher or install it as <code>drakeforge.d86</code> so the new tab is a
    full browsing context.
  </p>
</main>
</body>
</html>
